# Reversing a Singly Linked List In-Place

## Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Definition of a singly linked list node
struct ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode* next;
    ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(nullptr) {}
};

/**
 * Reverses a singly linked list in place.
 *
 * @param head  Pointer to the head of the linked list.
 * @return      Pointer to the new head of the reversed list.
 */
ListNode* reverseLinkedList(ListNode* head) {
    ListNode* prev = nullptr;   // Will become the new head
    ListNode* curr = head;      // Current node being processed
    ListNode* next = nullptr;   // Temporary pointer to save next node

    while (curr != nullptr) {
        next = curr->next;      // 1. Save the next node
        curr->next = prev;      // 2. Reverse the link
        prev = curr;            // 3. Move prev forward
        curr = next;            // 4. Move curr forward
    }

    // prev now points to the new head of the reversed list
    return prev;
}

// ---------- Utility: print list ----------
void printList(ListNode* head) {
    while (head != nullptr) {
        std::cout << head->val << " -> ";
        head = head->next;
    }
    std::cout << "nullptr" << std::endl;
}

// ---------- Demo ----------
int main() {
    // Build: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> nullptr
    ListNode* head = new ListNode(1);
    head->next = new ListNode(2);
    head->next->next = new ListNode(3);
    head->next->next->next = new ListNode(4);
    head->next->next->next->next = new ListNode(5);

    std::cout << "Original:  ";
    printList(head);

    head = reverseLinkedList(head);

    std::cout << "Reversed:  ";
    printList(head);

    // (Cleanup omitted for brevity; in production, delete all nodes)
    return 0;
}
```

## How It Works (Step-by-Step)

We use three pointers: `prev`, `curr`, and `next`.

| Step | `prev` | `curr` | `next` | Action |
|------|--------|--------|--------|--------|
| Init | `nullptr` | `1` | — | Start |
| 1 | `nullptr` | `1` | `2` | Save next, point `1->next = nullptr` |
| 2 | `1` | `2` | `3` | Point `2->next = 1` |
| 3 | `2` | `3` | `4` | Point `3->next = 2` |
| 4 | `3` | `4` | `5` | Point `4->next = 3` |
| 5 | `4` | `5` | `nullptr` | Point `5->next = 4` |
| End | `5` | `nullptr` | — | Return `prev` (= `5`) |

Result: `5 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> nullptr`

## Complexity Analysis

| Metric | Complexity | Explanation |
|--------|-----------|-------------|
| **Time** | **O(n)** | Each node is visited exactly once. We perform a constant amount of work (pointer reassignment) per node. |
| **Space** | **O(1)** | Only three pointer variables (`prev`, `curr`, `next`) are used, regardless of list length. No recursion stack or auxiliary data structures. |

### Why In-Place?

- **No new nodes** are allocated.
- **No copying** of data between nodes — only the `next` pointers are rewired.
- This makes it optimal in both time and space compared to approaches that create a new list (which would be O(n) space) or use recursion (which would be O(n) stack space).

## Alternative: Recursive Approach (for comparison)

```cpp
ListNode* reverseRecursive(ListNode* head) {
    if (head == nullptr || head->next == nullptr)
        return head;

    ListNode* newHead = reverseRecursive(head->next);
    head->next->next = head;
    head->next = nullptr;
    return newHead;
}
```

| Metric | Recursive | Iterative |
|--------|-----------|-----------|
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space | **O(n)** (call stack) | **O(1)** |

The **iterative approach is preferred** in production code because it uses constant extra space and avoids the risk of stack overflow on very long lists.

